WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.790
(dramatic music)

00:00:03.828 --> 00:00:06.460
- I think it's part of
who we are as a species

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.970
to ask the question of where are we,

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.840
where did we come from,
where are we going,

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.303
and are we going there alone?

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:20.650
The largest mirrors in
the world enable a chance

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.940
for all of us to answer those questions

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.463
that mean so much to us.

00:00:29.989 --> 00:00:32.950
(mysterious electronic music)

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.410
My name is Patrick McCarthy, and I'm

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.790
the principle astronomer behind the

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.260
Giant Magellan Telescope project,

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.170
the largest telescope in the world,

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.240
handcrafted, start to finish, here

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.916
at the University of Arizona.

00:00:42.916 --> 00:00:45.120
(plucky music)

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.450
The process of making
a mirror, in some sense,

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:48.730
it's like making a cake.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.350
First thing is you get
all the ingredients,

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.540
you have the glass,

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.890
you build the mold and
we put the glass on top,

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.523
and then you put it in the oven.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.560
It's then heated up to
such a high temperature

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.390
that the glass melts,
until follows into the mold

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.810
and makes the rough shape of your cake.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:10.083
Now in our case the
icing is the hard part.

00:01:11.784 --> 00:01:13.980
The mirrors weigh about 17 tons,

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.530
they're up to 8.4 meters in diameter.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.550
That's a really big piece of glass.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.450
Then to turn that piece of
glass into a final optic

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.350
takes another two to three years of

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:27.603
precision grinding and
polishing and testing.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.810
Each mirror, from start
to finish, takes roughly

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:35.060
seven years to produce and there's roughly

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.900
a hundred people working on it.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.510
So about 200 person years go into making

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:41.623
one of these mirrors.

00:01:44.474 --> 00:01:47.590
(gentle music)

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:49.420
The Giant Magellan Telescope will be sited

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:50.700
in the Chilean Andes.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.720
You have the sharpest images in Chile,

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.480
dry weather, clear skies,
and no artificial lights.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.230
It's a perfect place for astronomy.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.560
The challenge, of course, is how you handle

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.320
this beautiful precision piece of glass.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.180
So we lift it with suction cups.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.320
The mirrors are moved very carefully

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.133
because they are glass, after all.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:18.000
Telescopes are our vehicles of discovery,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.750
they're our ships in the universe,

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.040
and the heart of the
telescope is the mirror.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:23.590
It's the thing that collects the light

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.910
that's come from millions or
billions of light years away

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.370
and brings us that information

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.800
that can allow us to
answer those questions,

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.506
where did we come from, where
are we going, are we alone?

00:02:34.506 --> 00:02:37.423
(mysterious music)

00:02:41.207 --> 00:02:43.707
(tone chimes)

